2f4d033f2c9e98e6a14a5bc81aa82b53"},"seconds":11.441930294036865,"seed":1100247034626882213,"stop":"eos","text":"To evaluate \\(\\log_{\\sqrt{8}}(64\\sqrt{8})\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express the base and the argument in terms of a common base:**\n   - The base \\(\\sqrt{8}\\) can be written as \\(8^{1/2}\\).\n   - The argument \\(64\\sqrt{8}\\) can be written as \\(64 \\cdot 8^{1/2}\\).\n\n2. **Express 64 and 8 as powers of 2:**\n   - \\(64 = 2^6\\)\n   - \\(8 = 2^3\\)\n\n3. **Rewrite the base and the argument using these powers of 2:**\n   - \\(\\sqrt{8} = (2^3)^{1/2} = 2^{3/2}\\)\n   - \\(64\\sqrt{8} = 2^6 \\cdot 2^{3/2} = 2^{6 + 3/2} = 2^{15/2}\\)\n\n4. **Use the change of base formula for logarithms:**\n   - \\(\\log_{2^{3/2}}(2^{15/2}) = \\frac{\\log_2(2^{15/2})}{\\log_2(2^{3/2})}\\)\n\n5. **Simplify the logarithms:**\n   - \\(\\log_2(2^{15/2}) = \\frac{15}{2}\\)\n   - \\(\\log_2(2^{3/2}) = \\frac{3}{2}\\)\n\n6. **Divide the results:**\n   - \\(\\frac{\\log_2(2^{15/2})}{\\log_2(2^{3/2})} = \\frac{\\frac{15}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}} = \\frac{15}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = 5\\)\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\log_{\\sqrt{8}}(64\\sqrt{8})\\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:55"}
